WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.800
There are thousands of paintings&nbsp;
of Jesus in the history of art.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.760
But this is probably the strangest one of all.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.320
Look at the face of Jesus for a moment.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.520
His gaze is not directed at the bread he holds&nbsp;
in his hands. He is not looking at his disciples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:26.400
either. He is looking at us. Directly. With dark,&nbsp;
fixed eyes that can become quite unsettling.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.280
His face is not painted like the rest of&nbsp;
the figures either. His figure is blurred,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.600
wrapped in a kind of luminous haze,&nbsp;
with a halo of light burning behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.880
his head. As if we were looking at an apparition.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.000
When we look to the sides, we see the disciples&nbsp;
accompanying him raising their hands in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.640
astonishment. A servant, pale as a statue, stops&nbsp;
halfway. And in the right corner, among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:57.120
shadows, we find three figures dressed in clothes&nbsp;
from another era, watching the scene in silence.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.160
Everything in this image feels strange. The light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.680
the figures, and of course,&nbsp;
the way Jesus was painted.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.000
But once you learn the story behind this work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.520
and discover who the people&nbsp;
hidden in that corner really are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:16.640
you will understand why this is, probably,&nbsp;
the strangest painting of Jesus ever created.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.720
This work is called Christ and&nbsp;
the Disciples at Emmaus. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:26.880
painted by the French artist Pascal&nbsp;
Dagnan-Bouveret between 1896 and 1897,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.360
and today it is held at the Carnegie&nbsp;
Museum of Art in the United States.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.920
The scene depicts one of the most mysterious&nbsp;
moments in the entire New Testament,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.320
and although we have covered it in other videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.040
it is worth remembering what it is&nbsp;
about in order to understand the work.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.720
According to the Gospel of Luke, shortly after&nbsp;
the crucifixion, two disciples were walking toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:54.480
a village called Emmaus when a stranger joined&nbsp;
them on the road. For hours they walked together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.960
talking about the scriptures and about&nbsp;
everything that had happened in Jerusalem.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.520
Upon arriving at Emmaus, they invited&nbsp;
him to dinner. And it was at that moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.080
when that stranger they had spent hours&nbsp;
talking with took the bread, blessed it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.120
and revealed himself to them as the risen Jesus.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:16.320
According to the gospel, in that instant when&nbsp;
they recognized him, Jesus disappeared from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.600
the place. And that is probably the exact&nbsp;
moment Dagnan-Bouveret froze in this scene.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.960
If we look at the work in detail, we realize&nbsp;
that Christ in this painting occupies the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.920
center of the composition. He sits at the&nbsp;
table, with the broken bread in his hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.760
about to bless it. His figure&nbsp;
radiates light, and the glow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:41.520
illuminates his hair and his face, outlining&nbsp;
him against the open landscape behind him.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.080
Historians have pointed out a fascinating&nbsp;
technical detail about this light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.680
and it is that Dagnan-Bouveret painted the scene&nbsp;
looking toward the source of illumination, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:58.240
turns Christ almost into a luminous apparition.&nbsp;
Perhaps a reference to the Resurrection itself.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.880
And this decision is technically much more&nbsp;
difficult than it seems. Painting against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.520
the light means that everything in front of the&nbsp;
light source should appear dark, turned into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:13.360
silhouette. But Dagnan-Bouveret did exactly the&nbsp;
opposite with the figure of Christ. His white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:19.040
robe, his face, and his hands remain visible, as&nbsp;
if the light were not coming from the landscape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:25.600
behind him, but from his own body. Moreover, the&nbsp;
setting sun and the figure of Jesus merge into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:32.000
single glow, to the point that it is impossible&nbsp;
to tell where one ends and the other begins.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.360
Now, if we look around him, we see that the&nbsp;
reactions are divided. One of the disciples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.160
looks at him with astonishment and disbelief,&nbsp;
while the other leans toward his figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.400
captivated by seeing his master&nbsp;
once again. And right beside him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.470
the servant of the place stands petrified by&nbsp;
what her eyes are seeing. Her pale skin and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:50.560
stillness make her look almost like a statue,&nbsp;
stopping the plate she was carrying halfway.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.040
But the most curious thing about this work is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.360
that although these three figures are&nbsp;
the only ones mentioned in the Bible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:03.520
in this painting we see three completely unknown&nbsp;
figures on the right side of the composition.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.760
A man, a woman, and a child, dressed&nbsp;
in nineteenth-century clothes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.920
witnessing the scene in silence.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.360
Thanks to a preparatory study of&nbsp;
this work that still survives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.240
we know exactly who these three people are.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.520
They are Pascal Dagnan-Bouveret, his wife&nbsp;
Anne-Marie, and their son Jean. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:27.840
artist painted himself and his entire family&nbsp;
as witnesses to the supper at Emmaus. Perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.280
that is why their presence feels so mysterious in&nbsp;
the scene. Because we are looking at three people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:40.240
from the Paris of 1896, inserted into an event&nbsp;
that took place in Judea two thousand years ago.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.920
But the real question is:&nbsp;
why would he do such a thing?

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.360
The answer lies in the life of the artist himself.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.000
But before continuing with this fascinating&nbsp;
story and telling you the reason why the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.880
artist included himself in the painting, I&nbsp;
would like to remind you that if you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.680
like to have this work in your collection,&nbsp;
you can now purchase it from our website.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:07.360
In the description you will find our catalog with&nbsp;
more than 1,000 carefully selected historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.480
works so you can choose your favorites&nbsp;
and begin your own art collection at home.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.200
Shipping is completely free worldwide, and&nbsp;
by purchasing from the store you will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.320
supporting me enormously in continuing&nbsp;
to create videos like this every week.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.240
Dagnan-Bouveret was one of the most&nbsp;
successful painters in the France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.160
of his time. He was a student&nbsp;
of the great Jean-Léon Gérôme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:36.480
and became famous for his peasant scenes of an&nbsp;
almost photographic level of realism. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.840
he was one of the first artists to use&nbsp;
photography as a tool to build his compositions.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:48.800
Curiously, in the 1890s, his painting took a&nbsp;
radical turn toward the religious. Historians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.280
point out that this may have been the result&nbsp;
of the influence of his wife Anne-Marie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.880
who was a devout Catholic, and who&nbsp;
may have been the driving force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.840
behind Dagnan-Bouveret making faith&nbsp;
the center of his life and his art.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:08.080
By painting himself and his family into the scene,&nbsp;
Dagnan-Bouveret was not breaking tradition on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:13.600
whim. The decision to appear alongside his&nbsp;
family witnessing the revelation of Christ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:19.520
follows an idea that experts describe as the&nbsp;
personalization of religious experience. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.080
idea that the miracles of the gospel are&nbsp;
not distant events from a remote past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.440
but experiences that continue to happen,&nbsp;
here and now, for those who have faith.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.800
And if we think about it, that idea includes us&nbsp;
as well. Because when we stand in front of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:41.440
painting, we occupy the same place as the artist's&nbsp;
family. Becoming spectators of a miracle that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:46.640
according to the artist, continues to&nbsp;
happen before anyone willing to see it.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:51.280
When this painting was presented, it made&nbsp;
a profound impression. And to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.840
the level of success it achieved, we&nbsp;
only need to look at who bought it.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:01.520
Henry Clay Frick was the steel magnate of&nbsp;
Pittsburgh, a partner of Andrew Carnegie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.720
and one of the richest and most demanding art&nbsp;
collectors in the world. His personal collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:13.040
included works by Rembrandt, Vermeer, and Goya.&nbsp;
And when he saw this painting, he decided it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:19.840
to be his. Years later, he ended up donating it to&nbsp;
the Carnegie Museum, where it remains to this day.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:25.680
At that moment, Dagnan-Bouveret was at the top&nbsp;
of the art world. He had won the medal of honor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:32.560
at the Universal Exposition in Paris. In 1900 he&nbsp;
was elected a member of the Institut de France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:38.240
the highest honor a French artist could&nbsp;
aspire to. His works sold for enormous prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:42.240
and his name was mentioned among&nbsp;
the great living painters of Europe.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.320
But fate was ironic with him.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:51.680
A few years after his death in 1929, the art&nbsp;
world changed completely. The modern avant-gardes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:58.080
Impressionism, Cubism, and everything that&nbsp;
came after, swept away the academic art he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:04.960
represented. Twentieth-century critics dismissed&nbsp;
his entire generation, labeling it as outdated and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:11.440
sentimental art. And his name, which once filled&nbsp;
the salons of Paris, was practically forgotten.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:14.800
Today, very few people know this painting. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:18.240
even fewer know the secret of&nbsp;
the family hidden within it.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.640
Perhaps that is the greatest paradox&nbsp;
of this work. An artist who wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:28.240
paint himself into eternity, and who&nbsp;
ended up being forgotten by history.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.640
As always, I would love to read your&nbsp;
opinion about this painting in the comments.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:41.040
Thank you for watching the video until the&nbsp;
end, and I will see you in the next video.

